01:14:59.350 --> 01:15:00.790
You're live.

01:15:01.670 --> 01:15:02.470
>> Yeah,

01:15:02.790 --> 01:15:03.350
here we go.

01:15:05.555 --> 01:15:15.315
One question that came up when we had the last meeting was where is the Andover Lake District and where is the watershed? And so I can at least address

01:15:16.355 --> 01:15:16.675
those.

01:15:26.130 --> 01:15:27.250
So here we go.

01:15:28.370 --> 01:15:32.690
We're trying to improve the water quality in Andover Lake.

01:15:33.765 --> 01:15:37.205
And there's a couple of different reasons for doing this.

01:15:37.845 --> 01:15:40.005
You know the town is interested

01:15:40.005 --> 01:15:41.365
in this because

01:15:42.485 --> 01:15:44.885
if the water quality of the lake

01:15:46.750 --> 01:15:51.070
continues to degrade even though it's degrading ever so slightly,

01:15:51.870 --> 01:15:53.550
you know at some point the

01:15:54.670 --> 01:16:00.670
Department of Energy and Environmental Protection may come in and say, Andover, you need to put sewers in,

01:16:01.315 --> 01:16:02.835
which is very costly

01:16:03.315 --> 01:16:06.755
and not a lot of excitement about doing that right now.

01:16:08.195 --> 01:16:11.955
The good news for those of us who live around the lake is that

01:16:12.355 --> 01:16:16.275
with the town being enthused about us improving the water quality,

01:16:17.210 --> 01:16:23.450
we in the vicinity of the lake directly benefit from that and so that's certainly of interest

01:16:23.850 --> 01:16:24.970
to all of us.

01:16:26.650 --> 01:16:29.850
The lake regs that we're working on,

01:16:30.250 --> 01:16:30.810
and

01:16:31.050 --> 01:16:32.970
I'm

01:16:32.305 --> 01:16:38.305
not going to show you all the verbiage that goes along with it. What I'm trying to do is get the concept across

01:16:38.705 --> 01:16:41.985
to people so that when we make the verbiage

01:16:42.145 --> 01:16:44.945
match the concept, we've got regulations that work.

01:16:45.610 --> 01:16:48.330
Are modeled after those that are in existence,

01:16:48.650 --> 01:16:52.650
have been in existence for the past fifteen years or so in the town of Columbia.

01:16:54.170 --> 01:16:58.890
And John Bellini who works with the town of Columbia as well

01:17:00.155 --> 01:17:01.195
said that

01:17:01.515 --> 01:17:03.515
Columbia Lake was showing

01:17:03.595 --> 01:17:06.395
algae blooms and that sort of thing and

01:17:06.715 --> 01:17:09.195
after they implemented this one,

01:17:10.075 --> 01:17:14.155
the lake got better, got cleaner, they don't have algae bloom problems anymore

01:17:14.460 --> 01:17:23.340
and they did have a lot of support from the people that lived around the lake to go implement the regulations and so that would be nice too.

01:17:25.340 --> 01:17:32.335
Briefly, how do the contaminants get into the lake? Well as the rain comes down through atmosphere it picks up contamination and it

01:17:32.655 --> 01:17:33.615
gets ground

01:17:34.175 --> 01:17:37.374
storm water runoff going into the lake. So that's

01:17:37.935 --> 01:17:39.135
a big portion

01:17:39.695 --> 01:17:43.135
of the nitrogen and phosphorus that goes into the lake.

01:17:43.790 --> 01:17:45.150
And then we've also

01:17:45.390 --> 01:17:47.310
got some septic systems

01:17:47.390 --> 01:17:50.190
that due to the small lots

01:17:50.190 --> 01:17:52.190
immediately surrounding the lake,

01:17:52.590 --> 01:17:54.670
you are not in ideal systems

01:17:55.230 --> 01:17:58.750
there. So we'll talk a little bit about septic systems going on too.

01:18:00.465 --> 01:18:05.105
But the lake regulations would divide the Lake District and watershed into four zones.

01:18:05.265 --> 01:18:06.225
And so

01:18:06.465 --> 01:18:13.345
we start with zone A right around the lake, moving out from there, zone B, zone C and zone D as you get further away.

01:18:14.570 --> 01:18:19.610
And we'll put more strict measures in place the closer we are to the lake.

01:18:20.890 --> 01:18:23.050
So this shows that down at Andover

01:18:23.370 --> 01:18:24.090
in

01:18:24.330 --> 01:18:25.130
blue

01:18:25.450 --> 01:18:28.410
down here, this is the Lake District

01:18:28.755 --> 01:18:30.755
around Andover Lake.

01:18:32.675 --> 01:18:34.035
And then this is the

01:18:34.595 --> 01:18:36.835
overlay zone map

01:18:37.075 --> 01:18:38.035
that we

01:18:38.915 --> 01:18:39.395
generated

01:18:40.960 --> 01:18:41.919
to work with

01:18:42.240 --> 01:18:45.999
in conjunction with the regulations. And outlining in red

01:18:46.240 --> 01:18:46.959
is

01:18:47.600 --> 01:18:54.080
the outline of the watershed area and you can see an awful lot of our watershed resides in Columbia and Hebron.

01:18:55.275 --> 01:18:57.115
And then again going back,

01:18:57.275 --> 01:19:00.395
the blue line is the Lake District itself.

01:19:01.515 --> 01:19:04.715
And so if you look at the yellow right around the lake,

01:19:05.195 --> 01:19:06.955
that's what we refer to as Zone A.

01:19:07.600 --> 01:19:13.280
That's where we're going to try to make the most significant reduction in the runoff into the lake.

01:19:14.240 --> 01:19:19.360
And just the big picture, if somebody is going to go and make a significant improvement on their lot

01:19:20.475 --> 01:19:22.075
and they're in Zone A,

01:19:22.715 --> 01:19:27.115
we would like the end result to be that the water going into the lake

01:19:27.355 --> 01:19:37.540
has 10% less phosphorus and nitrogen in it than it did before the guy starts whatever addition he's gonna put on. So he's going to have to take measures that mitigate

01:19:37.700 --> 01:19:43.220
the water runoff so that the water going into the lake is cleaner than it used to be.

01:19:44.340 --> 01:19:46.180
And that's sometimes not an easy task.

01:19:47.805 --> 01:19:51.085
Zone B would be a reduction of 7.5%.

01:19:51.725 --> 01:19:53.005
Zone C,

01:19:53.405 --> 01:19:54.525
which is the purple,

01:19:54.845 --> 01:19:56.765
that would be a reduction of

01:19:57.485 --> 01:19:58.525
5%.

01:19:59.325 --> 01:20:01.405
And then Zone D, which is the brown,

01:20:02.180 --> 01:20:05.380
which is outside Lake District, it's still in the watershed,

01:20:05.780 --> 01:20:06.660
we would have

01:20:07.140 --> 01:20:08.900
no significant,

01:20:09.300 --> 01:20:12.820
we wouldn't have to use what we're going to see as a nitrogen allocation

01:20:12.820 --> 01:20:13.700
worksheet,

01:20:14.420 --> 01:20:16.980
but they would have to mitigate

01:20:17.295 --> 01:20:20.495
runoff from the increased impervious area

01:20:20.495 --> 01:20:21.535
that they have.

01:20:22.735 --> 01:20:23.775
So that's the map.

01:20:29.280 --> 01:20:30.480
Again, if you

01:20:31.600 --> 01:20:37.920
improve your property, one of the improvements should be improve the runoff into the lake. And as we talked about,

01:20:38.400 --> 01:20:40.480
the nutrient allocation worksheet,

01:20:40.480 --> 01:20:42.240
the reductions we just talked about there.

01:20:42.875 --> 01:20:43.835
Additionally,

01:20:43.915 --> 01:20:51.035
you must infiltrate the first two inch flush of storm water for volume of rain event from the newly increased surface area.

01:20:51.515 --> 01:20:53.835
So in the case of 151

01:20:53.835 --> 01:20:54.875
Lakeside Drive,

01:20:58.510 --> 01:21:01.550
putting a shed into place of 400 square feet,

01:21:02.110 --> 01:21:06.110
we would expect both a 10% reduction in runoff

01:21:06.110 --> 01:21:08.430
and then the ability to infiltrate

01:21:08.430 --> 01:21:09.630
the two inches

01:21:09.790 --> 01:21:16.895
of storm water running down off that impervious surface to go something to do something to mitigate that from

01:21:17.055 --> 01:21:22.575
running into the lake. And normally that would be a trench filled with stones or something like that so

01:21:22.575 --> 01:21:25.295
that it doesn't all just run off into the lake there.

01:21:27.050 --> 01:21:29.130
If you were doing a minor improvement

01:21:29.290 --> 01:21:34.250
and in Zone A it's 100 square feet, B is 200, C is 300,

01:21:34.570 --> 01:21:37.610
and then again in Zone D, you don't need to use the worksheet.

01:21:39.105 --> 01:21:44.545
Wouldn't require the use of the worksheet, but still requires mitigation of the two inch rainfall runoff.

01:21:45.025 --> 01:21:47.425
So that's what we're looking to do to

01:21:48.385 --> 01:21:49.265
capture

01:21:49.425 --> 01:21:51.425
the stormwater runoff into the lake.

01:21:55.910 --> 01:21:56.790
This is

01:21:57.110 --> 01:22:00.630
the first page of that nutrient allocation worksheet

01:22:00.630 --> 01:22:01.430
and

01:22:01.990 --> 01:22:04.630
as you can see it still

01:22:05.405 --> 01:22:12.765
refers to Columbia Lake Watershed at the top of the page there as opposed to Andover because you know we're just working

01:22:13.325 --> 01:22:17.565
on making sure that we understand this worksheet before we go

01:22:17.900 --> 01:22:19.899
make that part of our regulation.

01:22:20.540 --> 01:22:24.380
The other thing that's interesting to note here is this block here

01:22:24.700 --> 01:22:25.660
which has

01:22:27.020 --> 01:22:29.180
the factors associated

01:22:29.180 --> 01:22:29.820
with

01:22:29.980 --> 01:22:31.020
the different

01:22:32.435 --> 01:22:33.395
surfaces.

01:22:34.595 --> 01:22:37.475
So if we look down here, woodland,

01:22:37.635 --> 01:22:42.435
which is the original condition of land before anything's built on it or the mulch beds,

01:22:42.675 --> 01:22:44.835
that's got a coefficient of 0.1

01:22:45.060 --> 01:22:48.580
and it goes up to, you know, if we pave the driveway,

01:22:48.980 --> 01:22:50.100
1.5.

01:22:50.260 --> 01:22:52.499
So somebody who is

01:22:52.740 --> 01:22:57.459
looking to build onto their house or put up a shed or anything like that,

01:22:57.860 --> 01:23:00.180
we get all these numbers here

01:23:00.695 --> 01:23:01.975
and we

01:23:01.975 --> 01:23:05.495
put the number of square feet associated with each of these items in.

01:23:05.895 --> 01:23:07.735
We'd multiply those times

01:23:08.455 --> 01:23:09.175
this

01:23:09.415 --> 01:23:11.975
factor for the runoff coefficient

01:23:12.215 --> 01:23:17.310
and that gives you a number here. You add all this up and that's gonna give you the total

01:23:17.630 --> 01:23:18.590
phosphorus

01:23:18.590 --> 01:23:20.910
nitrogen runoff going into the lake.

01:23:21.390 --> 01:23:24.350
And that's before you start to do any remodeling.

01:23:26.190 --> 01:23:35.765
We would expect a 10% reduction of that and so that gives us the target that we're going to shoot for. So that's what this page does, that calculates

01:23:36.005 --> 01:23:40.085
what's going on originally so we know how much better we want to make the water.

01:23:43.940 --> 01:23:45.700
Here's the second page of that

01:23:46.100 --> 01:23:49.140
and it's going to talk about, okay, if we're going to make the

01:23:50.900 --> 01:23:51.860
water better,

01:23:52.180 --> 01:23:54.260
what are we going to do? And it's got,

01:23:55.275 --> 01:24:00.235
you know, we're going to change lawn to mulch bed and that shows an improvement

01:24:00.475 --> 01:24:01.195
there.

01:24:01.515 --> 01:24:03.595
And you've got all these different things,

01:24:04.235 --> 01:24:09.035
challenges to go through and show what are we doing to

01:24:09.500 --> 01:24:10.540
improve it.

01:24:11.020 --> 01:24:14.220
And then you get a load reduction when you tally all that up.

01:24:14.860 --> 01:24:19.500
And that's got to come and take you down to below that 10% improvement we were looking at.

01:24:22.005 --> 01:24:26.565
So there's still a couple of bugs that we're trying to work on as

01:24:26.565 --> 01:24:27.045
we

01:24:27.365 --> 01:24:29.445
figure out how to use this worksheet.

01:24:29.445 --> 01:24:33.605
But the worksheet is really the nuts and bolts of what we would be doing.

01:24:34.005 --> 01:24:38.459
If you're in zone B, it's a seven and a half percent reduction instead of zone A.

01:24:44.540 --> 01:24:50.220
Okay, so when when would you be required to use this nutrient allocation worksheet according to our regulations?

01:24:50.715 --> 01:24:55.595
If you're building a new house on a lot, you gotta use the worksheet.

01:24:56.395 --> 01:25:05.990
If you're improving more than 50% of the structure square footage within a ten year period, what we're trying to do there is make sure that we don't incrementally

01:25:05.990 --> 01:25:06.870
improve

01:25:08.150 --> 01:25:14.790
the structure on the lake so that we can avoid improving the water quality going into the lake. We want to make sure that

01:25:15.190 --> 01:25:18.230
if you're improving your lot, you're improving the water quality.

01:25:20.445 --> 01:25:22.125
Increasing the floor area

01:25:22.285 --> 01:25:23.005
and that

01:25:23.405 --> 01:25:26.605
varies by zone, most strictly in Zone A,

01:25:26.845 --> 01:25:29.325
the least restrictive in Zone C.

01:25:29.565 --> 01:25:31.325
If you add the accessory structure

01:25:31.965 --> 01:25:32.765
like

01:25:32.925 --> 01:25:33.485
a shed,

01:25:34.070 --> 01:25:36.230
again it varies by zone

01:25:36.470 --> 01:25:37.270
in

01:25:38.070 --> 01:25:44.870
increasing the impervious surface area, putting in a bigger driveway, paving a driveway, all those things, we get you there.

01:25:45.590 --> 01:25:48.550
So these are the things that would require use of that worksheet.

01:25:49.355 --> 01:25:56.715
As we said earlier on, if it was less than 100 square feet we were adding, then you wouldn't have to use the worksheet, you just have to

01:25:56.955 --> 01:25:59.835
accommodate the runoff from that particular

01:26:00.475 --> 01:26:00.955
surface.

01:26:06.490 --> 01:26:10.890
Moving on to septics, you know the Lake District, a lot of small lots

01:26:11.050 --> 01:26:11.850
with

01:26:12.410 --> 01:26:13.050
the

01:26:13.370 --> 01:26:16.584
small lots having wells and septic systems.

01:26:16.585 --> 01:26:18.745
The majority of the septic systems

01:26:19.225 --> 01:26:21.065
are pretty old at this point.

01:26:21.785 --> 01:26:23.545
A lot of them are undersized

01:26:24.345 --> 01:26:28.665
not meeting the minimum separation distance to groundwater or to the lake.

01:26:29.625 --> 01:26:30.265
And

01:26:30.910 --> 01:26:36.270
the existing septic systems are often located in the water table for at least a good portion of the year.

01:26:37.630 --> 01:26:42.510
These regulations would apply to Zone A and Zone B, the two zones that are closest

01:26:43.150 --> 01:26:43.950
to the lake.

01:26:48.155 --> 01:26:50.795
So the conditions required a new septic

01:26:51.915 --> 01:26:54.635
in those if you're increasing

01:26:55.675 --> 01:26:57.115
the use requiring

01:26:57.115 --> 01:27:02.690
a B100 approval under the Connecticut Public Health Code that requires a new septic septic system.

01:27:04.050 --> 01:27:08.610
Net floor area of the principal building increases by 20 or 30%

01:27:08.610 --> 01:27:10.530
for Zone A, Zone B,

01:27:10.850 --> 01:27:12.130
the new septic.

01:27:12.770 --> 01:27:15.570
Increasing the overall footprint of the principal structure

01:27:16.485 --> 01:27:18.485
by 25 or 30%

01:27:18.485 --> 01:27:25.205
for all the structures combined if you were adding a shed, an accessory apartment, anything like that to 40

01:27:25.205 --> 01:27:26.325
or 50%,

01:27:26.325 --> 01:27:28.485
again we'd be looking at

01:27:29.045 --> 01:27:31.045
the new septic system in there.

01:27:32.540 --> 01:27:35.020
Anytime we're adding one or more bedrooms

01:27:35.820 --> 01:27:40.460
and when greater than 50% of the principal structure square footage is rebuilt

01:27:41.020 --> 01:27:44.780
and there's any increase in living area requiring a B100 approval.

01:27:45.145 --> 01:27:46.025
I

01:27:46.025 --> 01:27:46.985
did get a

01:27:47.465 --> 01:27:52.584
email back from Art who couldn't join us tonight. He was asking me about this particular thing

01:27:52.985 --> 01:27:55.225
and I thought he had a good comment there.

01:27:58.090 --> 01:28:00.730
His comment was if you're tearing down a

01:28:00.890 --> 01:28:03.930
house and rebuilding it but you're not making it

01:28:04.650 --> 01:28:07.610
any bigger, don't we care about the septic system?

01:28:07.850 --> 01:28:08.330
And we do.

01:28:10.025 --> 01:28:17.385
So I've got to take a look at the wording on that one, but that would be our intention. You don't necessarily have to make it bigger.

01:28:17.625 --> 01:28:19.305
If you're going to rebuild your house,

01:28:21.385 --> 01:28:24.105
it might be a good time to redo your septic tank as well.

01:28:25.330 --> 01:28:29.090
And regulation is cumulative as of the date of the regulation.

01:28:29.090 --> 01:28:35.410
So again, if you do a little bit here, a little bit there, then you add up to meet these requirements,

01:28:35.650 --> 01:28:39.570
that's when we go in to take a look at the condition of your septic system.

01:28:42.855 --> 01:28:47.415
Somebody could say, well I just put in a new septic system a year or two ago.

01:28:48.295 --> 01:28:52.375
And so there are some exemptions. If you met one of those requirements to

01:28:52.550 --> 01:28:55.030
ensure that you have a good septic system,

01:28:55.510 --> 01:29:01.110
if you get an engineer in there and prove the existing system is greater than 12 inches above the max high water table,

01:29:03.590 --> 01:29:06.870
that might be a factor that would say yeah you're good to go.

01:29:07.685 --> 01:29:14.085
You're greater than 35 feet to the high water mark of the Andover Lake or tributary, the various streams that run into it,

01:29:14.485 --> 01:29:14.965
that

01:29:15.365 --> 01:29:16.485
might be okay.

01:29:17.925 --> 01:29:19.125
And the last one,

01:29:19.830 --> 01:29:21.590
I'm still a little fuzzy on

01:29:21.990 --> 01:29:29.590
what this means myself and so I got to do some more homework on that, exceeds 50% of the size requirements for a new principal

